+# Go, Rust, Lua and JavaScript: scoped engineering profiles
+
+Select only the language/runtime actually discovered. All commands below are
+conditional examples for an inspected, authorized project with the matching installed
+toolchain. Resolve target modules, feature flags, OS/architecture, package manager,
+lockfiles and CI policy first. Do not fetch dependencies or upgrade tools silently.
+Inspect build scripts, test setup, compiler plugins, native extensions and lifecycle
+hooks before executing them; "check" and "test" commands can execute project code.
+
+## Go
+
+Inspect `go.mod`, `go.sum`, `go.work` when present, the Go/toolchain directives, build
+tags, cgo, target GOOS/GOARCH, module boundaries and vendoring policy. Verify installed
+Go and whether toolchain selection would download another version.
+
+Use the actual module/package closure. Conditional checks include `gofmt -l` on
+selected files (preview only), `go vet ./...`, and `go test -count=1 ./...` for a fresh
+run instead of silently trusting cached test output. Use `go test -race -count=1 ./...`
+only on supported targets with required cgo/toolchain support and suitable fixtures.
+A race-free observed run is not proof of all interleavings. Respect the project's
+network/module policy; do not run `go mod tidy` or upgrade packages as a review step.
+
+Trace goroutine ownership, channel closure, cancellation/context deadlines, resource
+cleanup, shared maps, locks, transactions and idempotency. Review error propagation,
+nil interface behavior, HTTP lifecycle and trusted data ownership where relevant.
+Use actual benchmarks/profiles/pprof/trace for suspected CPU, memory or goroutine
+problems; static inspection does not produce a measured improvement.
+
+## Rust
+
+Inspect workspace manifests, `Cargo.lock`, `rust-toolchain.toml`/toolchain selection,
+MSRV, editions, target triples, feature/cfg combinations, build scripts and proc macros.
+Do not assume all features can be enabled together or that one target covers FFI.
+
+Conditional checks: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `cargo check --locked`,
+`cargo clippy --locked`, and `cargo test --locked`, narrowed to the real workspace/
+package/target when needed. Use the existing offline policy and cache; missing cached
+dependencies are BLOCKED, not permission to contact a registry. Add `-D warnings` only
+when the project's verified lint policy requires it. Formatting/type checks do not
+establish runtime correctness, and Cargo tests do not by themselves validate every
+feature combination or deployment artifact.
+
+Review ownership/borrowing/lifetimes, `Send`/`Sync`, lock scope across `.await`, task
+cancellation, backpressure, `Result`/error handling, panic boundaries and `Drop` cleanup.
+For `unsafe` and FFI inspect each safety invariant, pointer validity, aliasing, buffer
+bounds, alignment, ABI and lifetime owner. Optional Miri/sanitizers/fuzzing need actual
+support, applicable targets and scope; do not assert they ran or prove all safety.
+
+## Lua
+
+Determine PUC Lua version, LuaJIT, Luau or an embedded engine dialect before analysis.
+These are not interchangeable. Inspect module/search paths, host APIs, Lua C modules,
+LuaRocks/lock conventions if used, sandbox/environment behavior and test runner.
+Never evaluate an untrusted Lua file just to discover its configuration.
+
+For matching PUC Lua installations, `luac -p <actual-file.lua>` checks parsing without
+producing bytecode output; it is not a runtime or host-integration test. Use existing
+configured lua-language-server, luacheck, StyLua or Busted only when installed and
+compatible; no Lua 5.4 syntax assumptions on Lua 5.1/LuaJIT/Luau projects. Embedded
+host behavior requires tests in that host, not merely the standalone interpreter.
+
+Review nil versus false, table key/sequence behavior, metamethod side effects, global
+state, coroutine lifecycle, yielding across host/C boundaries, protected-call/error
+behavior and cleanup. Coroutine scheduling is not automatically parallel execution.
+Treat `load`, `loadfile`, `dofile`, package loaders and native bindings as executable
